1
00:00:16,676 --> 00:00:18,397
[WOMAN 1]: Yeah, I think he worked at, uh...

2
00:00:18,517 --> 00:00:19,818
[MURDOCK]: Pimlico he worked at?

3
00:00:22,180 --> 00:00:28,025
[BARNHART]: Well, I don't know about other ones, but he was here at the Fairtime and down in Charlestown.

4
00:00:28,045 --> 00:00:28,145
[WOMAN 1]: Yeah.

5
00:00:28,205 --> 00:00:32,928
[WOMAN 1]: Yeah, that's what he, uh...

6
00:00:33,569 --> 00:00:37,612
[WOMAN 1]: But I, he had something, because he had the nurse 24 hours a day.

7
00:00:38,613 --> 00:00:39,373
[WOMAN 2]: Who was that?

8
00:00:39,513 --> 00:00:40,414
[WOMAN 1]: Rube Marquard.

9
00:00:42,175 --> 00:00:44,377
[WOMAN 1]: But now both his school brothers are.

10
00:00:46,092 --> 00:00:47,933
[WOMAN 1]: college there, balls of fire.

11
00:00:48,153 --> 00:00:50,593
[WOMAN 1]: I don't know, I mean, I never know how old anybody is.

12
00:00:51,794 --> 00:00:54,394
[WOMAN 1]: Joey Sewell was just hither and thither and yon.

13
00:00:54,874 --> 00:00:59,176
[WOMAN 1]: He lives down in Alabama and he was up in Ohio the other weekend.

14
00:00:59,196 --> 00:01:06,458
[WOMAN 1]: Because we saw him down in Alabama when our son was stationed at Venice.

15
00:01:06,858 --> 00:01:10,579
[WOMAN 1]: We went down that way to see him after he got back from Germany.

16
00:01:13,419 --> 00:01:14,860
[MURDOCK]: What I'd like to do is to just

17
00:01:15,842 --> 00:01:22,686
[MURDOCK]: ask you about where you grew up and what your family did and how you got started in the baseball.

18
00:01:24,547 --> 00:01:26,488
[MURDOCK]: Are you from Hagerstown?

19
00:01:26,508 --> 00:01:29,990
[BARNHART]: No, I was up here in Pennsylvania, not very far.

20
00:01:31,891 --> 00:01:33,192
[BARNHART]: I grew up on a farm.

21
00:01:37,694 --> 00:01:40,876
[BARNHART]: I guess I got some of my baseball from my father.

22
00:01:41,636 --> 00:01:42,697
[BARNHART]: He used to play ball.

23
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:44,038
[BARNHART]: He had a little league ball around.

24
00:01:48,041 --> 00:01:50,722
[BARNHART]: He taught me a lot of baseball.

25
00:01:50,802 --> 00:01:55,123
[BARNHART]: I can remember him playing ball with me when I wore dresses then.

26
00:01:56,143 --> 00:02:02,684
[BARNHART]: Of course, you wore dresses then until you were old enough to go to school.

27
00:02:08,386 --> 00:02:09,826
[MURDOCK]: This birth date, right?

28
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:10,706
[MURDOCK]: December 29, 1895?

29
00:02:10,746 --> 00:02:11,246
[BARNHART]: That's right.

30
00:02:11,266 --> 00:02:11,887
[MURDOCK]: You're 85 now?

31
00:02:11,907 --> 00:02:12,467
[MURDOCK]: 84?

32
00:02:18,811 --> 00:02:21,473
[WOMAN]: He's not very good at math.

33
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:27,397
[MURDOCK]: Did you learn, did you do a lot of throwing on the farm there?

34
00:02:27,417 --> 00:02:29,439
[MURDOCK]: Did you have enough people to play games?

35
00:02:30,239 --> 00:02:33,502
[BARNHART]: I had a little team there in the little valley.

36
00:02:33,582 --> 00:02:36,204
[BARNHART]: I had a little team there.

37
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:42,268
[BARNHART]: I played on that until I started going to school.

38
00:02:42,288 --> 00:02:45,910
[BARNHART]: I went to Shippensburg over there.

39
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:47,532
[BARNHART]: Graduated there in 16.

40
00:02:49,628 --> 00:02:50,368
[MURDOCK]: Did they have a team?

41
00:02:51,789 --> 00:02:52,929
[BARNHART]: They had a team, yeah.

42
00:02:57,891 --> 00:03:01,212
[MURDOCK]: Somebody scout you at Shippensburg, or how did you break into pro ball?

43
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:08,214
[BARNHART]: Well, yeah, the Blue Ridge League had started that.

44
00:03:10,355 --> 00:03:13,656
[BARNHART]: I used to go to see them play every chance I got.

45
00:03:15,717 --> 00:03:19,478
[BARNHART]: In the summertime, I got on one of the teams.

46
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:21,391
[MURDOCK]: Which team do you remember?

47
00:03:21,651 --> 00:03:22,011
[BARNHART]: Frederick?

48
00:03:24,052 --> 00:03:25,572
[MURDOCK]: That'd be in 1960 or 17?

49
00:03:25,652 --> 00:03:28,033
[BARNHART]: That was in 15.

50
00:03:28,194 --> 00:03:31,635
[BARNHART]: I played with them.

51
00:03:31,815 --> 00:03:35,957
[BARNHART]: It was 1916, 1917, the league closed.

52
00:03:35,997 --> 00:03:38,138
[MURDOCK]: Because of the war, huh?

53
00:03:38,358 --> 00:03:43,500
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I went up to Williamsport and got on a team up there.

54
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:48,863
[BARNHART]: It was a Lycoming motor team.

55
00:03:51,643 --> 00:03:53,144
[BARNHART]: They played all the teams.

56
00:03:53,184 --> 00:03:58,668
[BARNHART]: They called the name of Hinchman, Bill Hinchman.

57
00:03:58,728 --> 00:04:01,990
[BARNHART]: He was managing that.

58
00:04:03,231 --> 00:04:03,991
[BARNHART]: I played under

59
00:04:18,194 --> 00:04:22,397
[BARNHART]: Well, I forget his name now, but he was with the Boston and Brooklyn Club.

60
00:04:22,977 --> 00:04:28,840
[BARNHART]: He wanted me to sign up with the Brooklyn Club for a tryout.

61
00:04:31,042 --> 00:04:39,807
[BARNHART]: So, Hinchman heard of this, and he came around and signed me up before the fellow did.

62
00:04:39,827 --> 00:04:41,488
[MURDOCK]: He was with Pittsburgh?

63
00:04:42,088 --> 00:04:42,548
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

64
00:04:42,769 --> 00:04:44,430
[MURDOCK]: He was scouting for Pittsburgh?

65
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:46,611
[BARNHART]: Yeah, he was scouting for Pittsburgh.

66
00:04:48,951 --> 00:04:50,012
[MURDOCK]: What'd he sign you up for?

67
00:04:51,232 --> 00:04:52,253
[BARNHART]: Me, what, the salary?

68
00:04:52,273 --> 00:04:53,133
[MURDOCK]: Yeah.

69
00:04:53,233 --> 00:04:54,894
[BARNHART]: I think it was $350.

70
00:04:55,655 --> 00:04:57,176
[MURDOCK]: That's pretty big money for you, wasn't it?

71
00:04:57,196 --> 00:04:58,737
[BARNHART]: Big money at that time, yeah.

72
00:05:00,418 --> 00:05:01,658
[MURDOCK]: What'd your father think of this?

73
00:05:02,259 --> 00:05:04,620
[MURDOCK]: Was he quite pleased you would be playing well?

74
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:05,160
[BARNHART]: He was pleased.

75
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:09,583
[BARNHART]: He always followed it.

76
00:05:09,663 --> 00:05:12,204
[BARNHART]: I had to pitch for the paper sometimes.

77
00:05:14,085 --> 00:05:16,027
[BARNHART]: Followed it all the time.

78
00:05:18,671 --> 00:05:20,612
[MURDOCK]: Do you have any boyhood heroes from Pittsburgh?

79
00:05:21,292 --> 00:05:22,193
[BARNHART]: Like Wagner?

80
00:05:23,973 --> 00:05:25,234
[BARNHART]: No, I knew Wagner.

81
00:05:25,654 --> 00:05:33,137
[BARNHART]: He was a coach on a team one year when I was up there in 1920.

82
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,621
[MURDOCK]: So where did you play in 1918?

83
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:41,701
[MURDOCK]: In Lycoming then?

84
00:05:43,202 --> 00:05:46,283
[BARNHART]: Lycoming.

85
00:05:46,743 --> 00:05:48,524
[MURDOCK]: And Pittsburgh signed you that?

86
00:05:48,893 --> 00:05:50,374
[MURDOCK]: after that season?

87
00:05:51,194 --> 00:05:55,256
[BARNHART]: Yeah, they shined me and sent me down to Birmingham.

88
00:05:57,357 --> 00:05:58,458
[MURDOCK]: How'd you like it down south?

89
00:05:59,038 --> 00:06:00,979
[BARNHART]: Well, life's alright.

90
00:06:01,039 --> 00:06:01,980
[BARNHART]: It was an awful hike.

91
00:06:04,141 --> 00:06:07,723
[MURDOCK]: Take any of those night train rides in the summer?

92
00:06:07,743 --> 00:06:08,863
[MURDOCK]: Must have been pretty warm, huh?

93
00:06:09,044 --> 00:06:09,504
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

94
00:06:12,085 --> 00:06:13,546
[MURDOCK]: Who was the manager of Birmingham?

95
00:06:19,222 --> 00:06:27,730
[BARNHART]: I just think of it now and then.

96
00:06:30,693 --> 00:06:33,195
[MURDOCK]: Were you playing outfield all these years?

97
00:06:33,315 --> 00:06:34,636
[BARNHART]: No, I played at the third base.

98
00:06:34,836 --> 00:06:37,219
[BARNHART]: Or infield, I played infield.

99
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:39,841
[MURDOCK]: How'd you happen to go to the outfield?

100
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:45,681
[BARNHART]: Well, I got to, when I went to Pittsburgh, I played third base for a couple years.

101
00:06:45,901 --> 00:06:51,923
[BARNHART]: So, Pie Traynor, he was a pretty good infielder.

102
00:06:53,324 --> 00:06:56,885
[BARNHART]: So, he switched me to the outfield, and I played the outfield.

103
00:07:00,046 --> 00:07:02,646
[MURDOCK]: Did you spend all of 1920 with Pittsburgh?

104
00:07:02,666 --> 00:07:05,927
[MURDOCK]: It says you played in 12 games that year.

105
00:07:07,008 --> 00:07:07,848
[BARNHART]: No, that's a year.

106
00:07:08,972 --> 00:07:12,593
[BARNHART]: He sent me out. When I came back and played 12 games at the end of the season.

XXX
[MURDOCK]: You hit .326 so you must have made a good debut there.

107
00:07:12,653 --> 00:07:15,333
[BARNHART]: I was in the '27 series, the '25 series.

XXX
[MURDOCK]: What was it like to go up to Pittsburgh there, as a, for the first time, be with some of these people?

108
00:07:35,682 --> 00:07:38,865
[BARNHART]: Well, I worked out with them in spring training, all of them, see.

109
00:07:42,709 --> 00:07:46,153
[BARNHART]: They just sent me down there just before the season opened.

110
00:07:47,714 --> 00:07:50,797
[MURDOCK]: Did you feel pretty confident you were going to be able to stick up there?

111
00:07:50,817 --> 00:07:51,998
[BARNHART]: Well, yes.

112
00:07:52,158 --> 00:07:55,502
[BARNHART]: I always had enough confidence.

113
00:07:55,862 --> 00:07:56,903
[MURDOCK]: How'd you do at Birmingham?

114
00:07:56,923 --> 00:07:58,185
[MURDOCK]: Must have had a pretty good year there, huh?

115
00:07:58,205 --> 00:07:59,206
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I had a good year there.

116
00:08:02,473 --> 00:08:05,396
[MURDOCK]: Now, who was manager at Pittsburgh when you came up there in 20?

117
00:08:05,536 --> 00:08:06,157
[BARNHART]: Gibson.

118
00:08:06,177 --> 00:08:06,777
[MURDOCK]: George Gibson.

119
00:08:07,658 --> 00:08:11,382
[MURDOCK]: Is he still active playing, or is he still catching then?

120
00:08:11,482 --> 00:08:13,644
[BARNHART]: Oh, he was catching then.

121
00:08:18,849 --> 00:08:21,432
[MURDOCK]: Remember some of the other players on the team when you first came up?

122
00:08:27,502 --> 00:08:28,662
[BARNHART]: You mean the Birmingham?

123
00:08:28,803 --> 00:08:30,483
[MURDOCK]: No, the Pittsburgh team.

124
00:08:30,883 --> 00:08:32,184
[MURDOCK]: Max Carey probably.

125
00:08:32,204 --> 00:08:34,044
[BARNHART]: Oh, Max Carey was there.

126
00:08:34,145 --> 00:08:34,645
[BARNHART]: Traynor.

127
00:08:35,745 --> 00:08:36,285
[BARNHART]: Rawlings.

128
00:08:38,546 --> 00:08:40,227
[BARNHART]: Big Ben, I think, was there.

129
00:08:44,648 --> 00:08:45,889
[BARNHART]: Schmidt, catcher.

130
00:08:47,349 --> 00:08:49,270
[MURDOCK]: That Earl Smith?

131
00:08:49,290 --> 00:08:52,031
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

132
00:08:52,071 --> 00:08:53,932
[BARNHART]: Babe Adams was one of the pitchers.

133
00:08:53,952 --> 00:08:54,092
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

134
00:08:54,152 --> 00:08:56,653
[MURDOCK]: Was Vic Aldridge with him?

135
00:09:06,376 --> 00:09:10,699
[MURDOCK]: Did they give you any kind of a salary boost for 1921?

136
00:09:10,739 --> 00:09:15,123
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I got a boost.

137
00:09:15,163 --> 00:09:17,265
[BARNHART]: I don't remember what it was or anything.

138
00:09:19,046 --> 00:09:20,428
[MURDOCK]: Did you play third base in 21?

139
00:09:20,488 --> 00:09:22,770
[MURDOCK]: That was before Traynor came up, I guess.

140
00:09:29,059 --> 00:09:31,800
[MURDOCK]: Who were some of the pitchers that gave you some trouble or that didn't?

141
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:33,301
[MURDOCK]: Remember Early Wynn?

142
00:09:34,921 --> 00:09:42,705
[BARNHART]: How about, well... Well, Vance, he's a fastball pitcher.

143
00:09:42,725 --> 00:09:50,968
[BARNHART]: Burleigh Grimes, he's a spit-baller, I believe that is.

144
00:09:59,290 --> 00:10:01,951
[BARNHART]: Quite a number of them, again, just thinking of names.

145
00:10:05,431 --> 00:10:06,991
[WOMAN 1]: Were you married at this time?

146
00:10:07,011 --> 00:10:07,532
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, yes.

147
00:10:08,652 --> 00:10:10,012
[WOMAN 1]: Did you travel with him?

148
00:10:10,812 --> 00:10:11,332
[WOMAN 2]: No.

149
00:10:12,613 --> 00:10:15,033
[WOMAN 2]: We weren't allowed to travel.

150
00:10:15,133 --> 00:10:17,753
[BARNHART]: Oh, you made several trips.

151
00:10:17,773 --> 00:10:23,475
[WOMAN 2]: Yeah, but we wasn't allowed to go to California or anything.

152
00:10:29,724 --> 00:10:34,285
[WOMAN 1]: So, did you live in Pittsburgh?

153
00:10:34,485 --> 00:10:39,886
[WOMAN 2]: No, no, I lived here in Pittsburgh.

154
00:10:40,086 --> 00:10:46,007
[WOMAN 1]: Oh, that made it nice, though, that you had your...

155
00:10:46,087 --> 00:10:51,088
[WOMAN 2]: I went to St. Louis one time, and they played there, and I was there.

156
00:10:52,529 --> 00:10:55,249
[WOMAN 2]: Morris and he forgot that he was there.

157
00:10:56,169 --> 00:10:56,790
[BARNHART]: John Morris.

158
00:11:00,514 --> 00:11:01,135
[MURDOCK]: Eddie Moore.

159
00:11:03,376 --> 00:11:04,457
[MURDOCK]: I've interviewed Eddie Moore.

160
00:11:05,738 --> 00:11:06,999
[MURDOCK]: He died a couple years ago.

161
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:11,402
[MURDOCK]: He was over in Ironton, Ohio, where he was living then.

162
00:11:12,963 --> 00:11:15,145
[MURDOCK]: And Carmen Hill, I've talked to him.

163
00:11:20,629 --> 00:11:23,211
[MURDOCK]: Hill and Meadows were the two that wore the glasses, huh?

164
00:11:27,282 --> 00:11:29,664
[MURDOCK]: What was this time you hit a home run off of?

165
00:11:29,704 --> 00:11:31,125
[MURDOCK]: Who was it you mentioned earlier?

166
00:11:31,705 --> 00:11:32,245
[BARNHART]: Markford?

167
00:11:32,265 --> 00:11:33,186
[MURDOCK]: Yeah, Markford.

168
00:11:33,646 --> 00:11:34,427
[MURDOCK]: Tell us about that.

169
00:11:36,208 --> 00:11:37,929
[BARNHART]: Well, he was a left-hand pitcher.

170
00:11:40,051 --> 00:11:41,512
[BARNHART]: I forget what inning it was.

171
00:11:43,013 --> 00:11:43,413
[BARNHART]: 8th or 9th.

172
00:11:43,513 --> 00:11:45,495
[BARNHART]: I guess it was the 9th inning.

173
00:11:47,056 --> 00:11:48,957
[BARNHART]: I just hit the home run and won the game.

174
00:11:57,140 --> 00:11:58,921
[WOMAN 1]: And what did you do in your off-season?

175
00:12:01,582 --> 00:12:03,023
[WOMAN 1]: Did you hunt or fish?

176
00:12:03,543 --> 00:12:05,104
[BARNHART]: Hunt.

177
00:12:05,184 --> 00:12:06,085
[BARNHART]: Hunt fish.

178
00:12:09,366 --> 00:12:12,628
[WOMAN 1]: That's what most of the fellas we talk to seem to do in their off-season.

179
00:12:13,308 --> 00:12:14,049
[WOMAN 1]: Hunt and fish.

180
00:12:14,669 --> 00:12:17,911
[WOMAN 2]: Well, now, yeah, but a lot of them have businesses now.

181
00:12:18,171 --> 00:12:20,212
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, now, yeah, but I mean in those older ones.

182
00:12:20,232 --> 00:12:25,755
[WOMAN 2]: He had to spend a year, was it a year, a year and a half, down at the pharmacy to fill a doctorate.

183
00:12:27,035 --> 00:12:30,177
[WOMAN 2]: And we just had one more year to go.

184
00:12:30,297 --> 00:12:39,282
[WOMAN 2]: And we tried encouraging him to go back and finish, but he couldn't go back.

185
00:12:46,846 --> 00:12:52,388
[WOMAN 1]: But there have been quite a few of the older players that we talked to.

186
00:12:52,428 --> 00:12:53,209
[WOMAN 1]: What did you do?

187
00:12:53,609 --> 00:12:55,330
[WOMAN 1]: Well, I fished and golfed.

188
00:12:56,115 --> 00:12:57,556
[WOMAN 1]: or I fished and hunted.

189
00:12:58,537 --> 00:13:00,338
[WOMAN 1]: Hunting, uh, Ed Rouse.

190
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:07,603
[WOMAN 1]: And Marquardt, of course, said he was on the vaudeville stage, so, on the off-season.

191
00:13:07,703 --> 00:13:12,646
[WOMAN 1]: And he went to the vaudeville circuit, I guess.

192
00:13:12,666 --> 00:13:13,707
[WOMAN 1]: That's what he said.

193
00:13:16,469 --> 00:13:18,570
[WOMAN 2]: And I hear from Johnny Morrison's wife.

194
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:21,252
[WOMAN 2]: She likes to go home with him.

195
00:13:22,113 --> 00:13:22,573
[WOMAN 2]: He died.

196
00:13:22,593 --> 00:13:23,874
[WOMAN 2]: He had diabetes, I think.

197
00:13:24,034 --> 00:13:25,155
[MURDOCK]: I didn't know that.

198
00:13:26,089 --> 00:13:29,070
[MURDOCK]: I hadn't really died, I didn't know.

199
00:13:35,252 --> 00:13:40,654
[MURDOCK]: But tell me about the 1927 season and Kai Kai Cuyler.

200
00:13:43,975 --> 00:13:44,676
[MURDOCK]: What happened there?

201
00:13:47,497 --> 00:13:48,177
[MURDOCK]: Can't we talk?

202
00:13:48,497 --> 00:13:49,317
[WOMAN 1]: Bang, bang, bang.

203
00:13:49,337 --> 00:13:50,818
[WOMAN 1]: That's the way it went.

204
00:13:55,606 --> 00:13:56,566
[BARNHART]: Didn't amount to much.

205
00:13:58,427 --> 00:14:02,368
[BARNHART]: Of course, the Yankees, they were way ahead, you know.

206
00:14:02,388 --> 00:14:06,910
[BARNHART]: We had to play up to the last game to win the series.

207
00:14:07,250 --> 00:14:07,910
[BARNHART]: The pennant, even.

208
00:14:08,610 --> 00:14:13,072
[BARNHART]: And of course, the Yankees, they just beat us four straight.

209
00:14:14,772 --> 00:14:15,713
[MURDOCK]: That wild pitch.

210
00:14:17,733 --> 00:14:18,553
[MURDOCK]: It ended it all.

211
00:14:18,894 --> 00:14:23,235
[MURDOCK]: Bill, did you say much after that?

212
00:14:27,371 --> 00:14:33,316
[MURDOCK]: What I was referring to was when Donnie Bush benched Cuyler about midway through the season and put you in place.

213
00:14:34,257 --> 00:14:38,641
[MURDOCK]: And Cuyler was, wasn't he having a pretty good year or so?

214
00:14:38,661 --> 00:14:39,762
[MURDOCK]: And never played him again?

215
00:14:39,782 --> 00:14:40,483
[MURDOCK]: Was that what happened?

216
00:14:41,764 --> 00:14:42,825
[BARNHART]: I don't know what it was.

217
00:14:42,905 --> 00:14:48,250
[BARNHART]: They didn't get along too good together.

218
00:14:48,270 --> 00:14:49,331
[BARNHART]: I guess for the reason.

219
00:14:50,936 --> 00:14:54,737
[MURDOCK]: But here's a star hitter and he didn't play him at all or in the World Series.

220
00:14:56,078 --> 00:14:59,279
[MURDOCK]: I asked Glenn Wright about that and he said, well, he didn't know either.

221
00:14:59,299 --> 00:15:00,099
[MURDOCK]: There was some feeling.

222
00:15:00,119 --> 00:15:04,381
[MURDOCK]: But he said he put Clyde Barnhart in there instead and that was a pretty good replacement.

223
00:15:05,922 --> 00:15:07,182
[MURDOCK]: So they didn't really miss him.

224
00:15:09,183 --> 00:15:12,324
[MURDOCK]: Because you finished up hitting .319 that year, 27.

225
00:15:12,344 --> 00:15:20,567
[WOMAN 1]: He a great year then, did he?

226
00:15:21,513 --> 00:15:22,914
[WOMAN 1]: with high batting averages.

227
00:15:22,954 --> 00:15:25,936
[WOMAN 1]: He did.

228
00:15:25,956 --> 00:15:33,262
[MURDOCK]: Do you have any favorite pitches you like to hit, or did you hit pretty much anything they threw up there?

229
00:15:35,083 --> 00:15:42,749
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I never had much trouble hitting.

230
00:15:42,889 --> 00:15:46,131
[MURDOCK]: In 1926, you only played 76 games, and your average went down.

231
00:15:46,151 --> 00:15:47,072
[MURDOCK]: Were you injured that year?

232
00:15:47,572 --> 00:15:49,554
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I was sick part of the time.

233
00:16:06,877 --> 00:16:10,039
[MURDOCK]: What manager did you like best?

234
00:16:10,719 --> 00:16:12,160
[MURDOCK]: Anybody stand out?

235
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:17,843
[BARNHART]: I guess Bush was... McKechnie was a good manager.

236
00:16:26,381 --> 00:16:31,302
[MURDOCK]: Charlie Grimm, a pretty lively person?

237
00:16:31,542 --> 00:16:32,042
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

238
00:16:33,622 --> 00:16:34,723
[MURDOCK]: Keeping everybody loose?

239
00:16:34,903 --> 00:16:35,903
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

240
00:16:39,304 --> 00:16:42,984
[MURDOCK]: Got any stories about him you can remember, Charlie?

241
00:16:44,385 --> 00:16:51,926
[BARNHART]: Oh, we had a little, kind of an orchestra in Morris, and Grimm and I, a banjo, and mandolin.

242
00:16:53,106 --> 00:16:53,927
[MURDOCK]: Which did you play?

243
00:16:54,487 --> 00:16:55,127
[BARNHART]: Mandolin.

244
00:16:59,510 --> 00:17:01,931
[MURDOCK]: When would you perform this orchestra?

245
00:17:03,552 --> 00:17:08,034
[MURDOCK]: Sure, in the clubhouse there you mean?

246
00:17:08,074 --> 00:17:13,737
[BARNHART]: Well, in spring training we played out in California in the theater there in the evenings sometimes.

247
00:17:13,897 --> 00:17:16,578
[BARNHART]: We had a little theater there.

248
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:26,423
[BARNHART]: And we practiced, we practiced on the road, on the rooms, or in our rooms.

249
00:17:30,195 --> 00:17:31,736
[BARNHART]: in the clubhouse sometimes.

250
00:17:31,756 --> 00:17:39,561
[MURDOCK]: What kind of a chap was Max Carey?

251
00:17:42,283 --> 00:17:44,024
[MURDOCK]: What kind of a chap was Max Carey?

252
00:17:44,384 --> 00:17:46,105
[BARNHART]: He was pretty good looking.

253
00:17:46,145 --> 00:17:50,288
[MURDOCK]: Was he a leader on the field, was he?

254
00:17:50,889 --> 00:17:54,271
[BARNHART]: Well, he was a base stealer, you know.

255
00:17:58,565 --> 00:18:00,746
[BARNHART]: He tried to teach the fellas to run the bases.

256
00:18:03,868 --> 00:18:06,589
[MURDOCK]: How'd you get along with Barney Dreyfuss?

257
00:18:07,749 --> 00:18:09,770
[BARNHART]: Oh, Barney Dreyfuss was all right.

258
00:18:11,051 --> 00:18:13,152
[MURDOCK]: Eddie Moore said he had all kinds of problems with him.

259
00:18:15,373 --> 00:18:17,314
[MURDOCK]: I just wondered if maybe it was Dreyfuss or if it was Moore.

260
00:18:19,115 --> 00:18:25,018
[WOMAN 2]: It wasn't Dreyfuss because when... where were they overseas someplace?

261
00:18:25,198 --> 00:18:27,079
[WOMAN 2]: Stony Cove, under the sun.

262
00:18:29,523 --> 00:18:39,108
[MURDOCK]: I guess his Eddie Moore was a little hot-headed, maybe, and that's some of the friction, because he did sell him off to Boston after the

263
00:18:58,779 --> 00:18:59,879
[MURDOCK]: 25 season, I guess.

264
00:19:00,019 --> 00:19:01,960
[MURDOCK]: He had a good season there.

265
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:09,541
[MURDOCK]: I think there's somebody else coming up, too.

266
00:19:10,501 --> 00:19:12,142
[MURDOCK]: George Grantham on the team when you were there?

267
00:19:12,162 --> 00:19:12,462
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

268
00:19:15,162 --> 00:19:15,802
[BARNHART]: Harris.

269
00:19:16,002 --> 00:19:18,163
[BARNHART]: We got Harris from Washington.

270
00:19:19,503 --> 00:19:21,524
[MURDOCK]: He was the one that was gassed in the war, is that right?

271
00:19:21,844 --> 00:19:22,164
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

272
00:19:26,665 --> 00:19:28,565
[MURDOCK]: Was Earl Smith a pretty lively chap.

273
00:19:29,002 --> 00:19:31,023
[BARNHART]: Yeah, pretty lively.

274
00:19:35,385 --> 00:19:36,826
[BARNHART]: He used to get a lot of arguments.

275
00:19:40,168 --> 00:19:46,632
[BARNHART]: Other teams, most of them managers, you'd either like McGraw or someone like that.

276
00:19:48,413 --> 00:19:48,953
[BARNHART]: Puggins.

277
00:19:55,216 --> 00:19:56,297
[MURDOCK]: How'd you like the umpires?

278
00:19:57,357 --> 00:19:58,318
[BARNHART]: Well, they was all right.

279
00:20:00,015 --> 00:20:01,076
[MURDOCK]: You ever get thrown out of a game?

280
00:20:03,057 --> 00:20:04,418
[MURDOCK]: What happened there?

XXX
[BARNHART]: He just called me out on a bad ball and I tossed my bat up in the air.

281
00:20:04,438 --> 00:20:05,618
[MURDOCK]: That's automatic, huh?

282
00:20:05,638 --> 00:20:09,221
[MURDOCK]: You think the umpires do a pretty good job in your experience?

283
00:20:31,277 --> 00:20:33,017
[BARNHART]: Well, lately, I don't know.

284
00:20:33,037 --> 00:20:35,658
[BARNHART]: They got so much trouble of late.

285
00:20:36,658 --> 00:20:37,519
[MURDOCK]: When you were playing?

286
00:20:37,939 --> 00:20:40,159
[BARNHART]: Oh, well, they did all right.

287
00:20:40,219 --> 00:20:43,940
[BARNHART]: They never had the arguments they do now.

288
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:51,502
[WOMAN 1]: Well, I know, but look what you've got playing ball now, with all these little temperamental people.

289
00:21:00,508 --> 00:21:04,210
[MURDOCK]: Did you play center field most of the time?

290
00:21:04,290 --> 00:21:05,570
[BARNHART]: No, mostly left.

291
00:21:05,910 --> 00:21:07,571
[BARNHART]: Right field, once in a while.

292
00:21:08,751 --> 00:21:09,732
[BARNHART]: Never played center much.

293
00:21:09,752 --> 00:21:12,713
[MURDOCK]: You have a pretty good arm, I imagine, didn't you?

294
00:21:12,833 --> 00:21:13,293
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

295
00:21:13,313 --> 00:21:16,714
[MURDOCK]: Did you get many assists from the outfield?

296
00:21:17,114 --> 00:21:17,574
[BARNHART]: A few.

297
00:21:18,675 --> 00:21:19,855
[MURDOCK]: That's quite an achievement.

298
00:21:23,096 --> 00:21:25,717
[MURDOCK]: Did you watch the All-Star game the other night?

299
00:21:25,777 --> 00:21:27,478
[MURDOCK]: Dave Parker made those two assists.

300
00:21:30,972 --> 00:21:31,593
[MURDOCK]: Right over the hill.

301
00:21:31,613 --> 00:21:36,896
[MURDOCK]: Did you do a lot of bunting?

302
00:21:38,517 --> 00:21:39,098
[BARNHART]: I bunted.

303
00:21:39,958 --> 00:21:41,019
[BARNHART]: Not too awful much.

304
00:21:41,179 --> 00:21:45,262
[BARNHART]: I never had any trouble learning to bunt.

305
00:21:45,322 --> 00:21:48,144
[BARNHART]: I learned to bunt while I was going to school.

306
00:21:48,164 --> 00:21:51,526
[MURDOCK]: Did anybody teach you or just teach yourself?

307
00:21:53,267 --> 00:21:53,547
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

308
00:21:53,567 --> 00:21:55,369
[BARNHART]: Coach at the college taught me.

309
00:21:57,170 --> 00:22:00,032
[MURDOCK]: When you came up to the big league or even with

310
00:22:02,146 --> 00:22:05,727
[MURDOCK]: down with Frederick there, did you get much help from some of the older players?

311
00:22:06,468 --> 00:22:07,568
[BARNHART]: Well, not too much.

312
00:22:07,848 --> 00:22:13,170
[MURDOCK]: They kind of, they figure you're trying to take a job away from them and they don't help you much, huh?

313
00:22:13,190 --> 00:22:13,330
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

314
00:22:14,211 --> 00:22:20,373
[BARNHART]: They was players, you know, that had been let go from other places, other teams.

315
00:22:33,270 --> 00:22:38,253
[BARNHART]: First of all, the balls that you're playing with now are rubber balls.

316
00:22:38,793 --> 00:22:40,094
[BARNHART]: What we had to play with.

317
00:22:42,375 --> 00:22:44,196
[MURDOCK]: You mean they're much more lively, huh?

318
00:22:44,276 --> 00:22:44,656
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

319
00:22:45,016 --> 00:22:47,237
[MURDOCK]: Weren't they pretty lively in your time, though?

320
00:22:47,277 --> 00:22:49,178
[MURDOCK]: Ruth hit all those home runs?

321
00:22:49,338 --> 00:22:52,700
[BARNHART]: Well, Ruth's about the only one.

322
00:22:53,140 --> 00:22:58,523
[BARNHART]: Well, it was like hitting a rock.

323
00:22:59,124 --> 00:22:59,764
[BARNHART]: Ball then.

324
00:23:02,361 --> 00:23:05,863
[BARNHART]: They never played much for a big inning like they do now.

325
00:23:05,903 --> 00:23:10,506
[BARNHART]: They always bunted mostly and played for the one run.

326
00:23:11,586 --> 00:23:13,047
[MURDOCK]: They don't know how to bunt today, really.

327
00:23:14,168 --> 00:23:19,111
[MURDOCK]: I think when they're supposed to bunt, it seems to me like they deliberately miss it so they can hit away.

328
00:23:20,511 --> 00:23:25,094
[BARNHART]: Well, so many of them bunt, they hold it back with both hands in the end.

329
00:23:25,114 --> 00:23:28,996
[BARNHART]: I was taught to bunt, and I always slipped this hand out.

330
00:23:32,827 --> 00:23:36,069
[BARNHART]: and bat and bat.

331
00:23:36,089 --> 00:23:41,171
[BARNHART]: Together, they just bat and hit the ball up in the air and every place else.

332
00:23:47,455 --> 00:23:50,036
[BARNHART]: I used to hit a lot of long fly balls.

333
00:24:02,428 --> 00:24:04,630
[BARNHART]: that would be home runs now.

334
00:24:06,291 --> 00:24:10,013
[BARNHART]: I scored three men from second base on sacrifice flies.

335
00:24:10,074 --> 00:24:15,738
[BARNHART]: Carey twice and Cuyler once.

336
00:24:15,798 --> 00:24:17,079
[MURDOCK]: Is that right?

337
00:24:17,099 --> 00:24:20,081
[MURDOCK]: Must be some kind of a record.

338
00:24:22,042 --> 00:24:24,224
[MURDOCK]: Was it your deep center field that hit the ball?

339
00:24:24,284 --> 00:24:25,965
[BARNHART]: Yeah, mostly center.

340
00:24:27,446 --> 00:24:29,908
[MURDOCK]: Of course, you had a big ballpark to bat in too, didn't you?

341
00:24:31,694 --> 00:24:35,836
[MURDOCK]: The deepest center field there is.

342
00:24:36,097 --> 00:24:38,158
[MURDOCK]: Did you like to play there?

343
00:24:38,178 --> 00:24:39,679
[MURDOCK]: Was it a good fielder's park?

344
00:24:39,839 --> 00:24:41,540
[MURDOCK]: It wasn't very good for hitters.

345
00:24:41,860 --> 00:24:48,044
[MURDOCK]: Where did the team stay?

346
00:24:48,844 --> 00:24:52,686
[MURDOCK]: When you're in Pittsburgh, did you have a rooming house or did you stay in a hotel?

347
00:24:52,846 --> 00:24:55,408
[BARNHART]: You had to furnish your own room.

348
00:24:57,542 --> 00:25:00,044
[MURDOCK]: You could stay with him then when he was in Pittsburgh?

349
00:25:00,144 --> 00:25:04,908
[WOMAN 2]: Oh yeah, I used to take a couple of kids along.

350
00:25:04,929 --> 00:25:12,035
[WOMAN 2]: We stayed in the boarding house one time, and then we had a residence.

351
00:25:13,316 --> 00:25:16,218
[MURDOCK]: It's kind of hard when school's still in to take the kids along.

352
00:25:16,238 --> 00:25:17,760
[WOMAN 2]: Well, I never stayed that long.

353
00:25:17,780 --> 00:25:19,581
[WOMAN 2]: I only stayed when they were home.

354
00:25:19,982 --> 00:25:22,304
[WOMAN 2]: In Pittsburgh, I didn't stay long.

355
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:30,981
[WOMAN 2]: Like, when they were home, I'd just drive up, and then they went on a trip, and then I'd come home.

356
00:25:32,921 --> 00:25:34,122
[WOMAN 1]: And took the children with you?

357
00:25:34,322 --> 00:25:35,902
[WOMAN 2]: No, I didn't ever took them all.

358
00:25:36,102 --> 00:25:36,322
[WOMAN 1]: Oh.

359
00:25:37,542 --> 00:25:38,282
[WOMAN 2]: I took two.

360
00:25:38,342 --> 00:25:46,484
[WOMAN 2]: I took the boys, and I never allowed them out.

361
00:25:46,604 --> 00:25:50,344
[WOMAN 2]: I guess when one boy was out, the others weren't.

362
00:25:50,364 --> 00:25:53,865
[WOMAN 1]: How many children do you have?

363
00:25:54,950 --> 00:25:55,450
[WOMAN 2]: Five.

364
00:26:00,815 --> 00:26:03,457
[WOMAN 1]: Do they live around here?

365
00:26:04,238 --> 00:26:05,899
[WOMAN 2]: No, one girl lives in Miami.

366
00:26:06,619 --> 00:26:07,600
[WOMAN 2]: I mean, in Florida.

367
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:09,322
[WOMAN 2]: She lives in Hialeah Garden.

368
00:26:12,784 --> 00:26:17,828
[WOMAN 2]: The rest of them are all around, depending on, you know, anything.

369
00:26:17,868 --> 00:26:20,991
[MURDOCK]: Have you ever had any contract disputes?

370
00:26:22,973 --> 00:26:23,233
[BARNHART]: No.

371
00:26:25,606 --> 00:26:31,550
[MURDOCK]: Were they pretty generous, do you think?

372
00:26:31,570 --> 00:26:35,032
[BARNHART]: Yeah, they were pretty generous.

373
00:26:35,132 --> 00:26:44,998
[BARNHART]: I got on average about everybody.

374
00:26:46,419 --> 00:26:48,441
[MURDOCK]: Did you feel spring training was necessary?

375
00:26:49,921 --> 00:26:52,383
[MURDOCK]: Eddie Rouse used to hold out, so he wouldn't go to spring training.

376
00:26:53,554 --> 00:26:55,895
[MURDOCK]: And you'd come and start hitting as well as ever.

377
00:26:55,935 --> 00:26:57,995
[MURDOCK]: Maybe that's because you were so good.

378
00:26:58,035 --> 00:27:00,556
[MURDOCK]: But do you think it really helped you get into shape?

379
00:27:00,676 --> 00:27:01,636
[BARNHART]: Oh, yes.

380
00:27:01,676 --> 00:27:03,257
[BARNHART]: The last time spring training.

381
00:27:03,277 --> 00:27:06,778
[BARNHART]: Of course some of these stars, like Roush.

382
00:27:08,938 --> 00:27:11,339
[BARNHART]: Didn't need too much to get into shape.

383
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:17,741
[MURDOCK]: You probably kept in pretty good shape in the off season with hunting and so on.

384
00:27:17,761 --> 00:27:20,361
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I never took on much weight.

385
00:27:21,362 --> 00:27:22,162
[BARNHART]: Wasn't hard to get down.

386
00:27:23,156 --> 00:27:27,599
[MURDOCK]: Were you eager to get going next spring?

387
00:27:27,719 --> 00:27:34,163
[MURDOCK]: Did you really look forward to it?

388
00:27:34,463 --> 00:27:39,727
[MURDOCK]: Did any big thrills stand out in your mind as you look back on your baseball career?

389
00:27:42,528 --> 00:27:46,671
[MURDOCK]: Anything particularly outstanding that you had happen to you or that you did?

390
00:27:48,072 --> 00:27:51,174
[MURDOCK]: Maybe those sacrifice flies, they were something.

391
00:27:55,624 --> 00:28:00,728
[BARNHART]: I remember when Pershing was out there at Pittsburgh one time.

392
00:28:00,788 --> 00:28:03,350
[BARNHART]: I had to play a right fielder.

393
00:28:03,470 --> 00:28:07,213
[BARNHART]: It was in the ninth inning.

394
00:28:08,514 --> 00:28:15,019
[BARNHART]: The ball hit it out to me, and I went over towards the line and jumped and caught it with one hand.

395
00:28:15,039 --> 00:28:21,044
[BARNHART]: That was the last out, and then it was over.

396
00:28:22,360 --> 00:28:23,981
[WOMAN 2]: General Pershing were you talking about?

397
00:28:24,001 --> 00:28:24,802
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

398
00:28:25,102 --> 00:28:29,805
[BARNHART]: And they had a piece of the paper about it.

399
00:28:29,825 --> 00:28:33,668
[BARNHART]: Pershing remarked about the catch down there.

400
00:28:33,808 --> 00:28:47,197
[BARNHART]: In the 25 Series, I

401
00:28:49,386 --> 00:28:57,468
[BARNHART]: I won the game that Washington had us beat, in the sixth game.

402
00:28:58,768 --> 00:29:00,129
[BARNHART]: They'd won that the Series would have been over.

403
00:29:00,249 --> 00:29:03,010
[MURDOCK]: I forgot about that.

404
00:29:03,030 --> 00:29:05,450
[BARNHART]: Kovalewski, he was a spitball pitcher.

405
00:29:07,051 --> 00:29:12,152
[BARNHART]: He had a spitball pitcher here in the Blue Ridge League, and I always liked him.

406
00:29:13,987 --> 00:29:15,608
[BARNHART]: I remember hitting the lid off of him.

407
00:29:15,668 --> 00:29:19,110
[BARNHART]: He won my sixth game and brought the series back to Pittsburgh.

408
00:29:19,190 --> 00:29:19,931
[BARNHART]: And that's the end of it.

409
00:29:20,912 --> 00:29:22,933
[BARNHART]: We won it the next day.

410
00:29:23,073 --> 00:29:23,813
[BARNHART]: Seventh game.

411
00:29:24,314 --> 00:29:25,615
[MURDOCK]: Did Johnson pitch that game?

412
00:29:26,135 --> 00:29:26,455
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

413
00:29:28,897 --> 00:29:32,119
[MURDOCK]: Did you hit any of those ground balls that Roger Peckinpah fumbled?

414
00:29:33,220 --> 00:29:35,061
[BARNHART]: No, I didn't hit any.

415
00:29:36,342 --> 00:29:38,443
[MURDOCK]: He set a series record of eight errors, you know.

416
00:29:38,563 --> 00:29:38,823
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

417
00:29:41,982 --> 00:29:45,363
[MURDOCK]: He'd been voted the most valuable player in the league just before the World Series.

418
00:29:45,383 --> 00:29:49,664
[MURDOCK]: And after he made eight errors, a number of people said they should hold the voting after the series.

419
00:29:51,024 --> 00:29:56,585
[MURDOCK]: But he was quite, I spent an evening with him one day and talked to him about those errors.

420
00:29:56,685 --> 00:30:09,328
[MURDOCK]: And he said that the Pittsburgh scorers were prejudiced against him because they wanted, see, Honus Wagner had the record of six errors by a shortstop in a World Series game.

421
00:30:10,143 --> 00:30:15,205
[MURDOCK]: And the Pittsburgh Scores wanted Wagner to break that, wanted that record broken so it wouldn't be with Wagner.

422
00:30:15,245 --> 00:30:19,427
[MURDOCK]: So they gave Peckinpah some errors which he felt he didn't deserve.

423
00:30:21,067 --> 00:30:25,329
[MURDOCK]: I guess some of the days the field was a little muddy and the ball was a little heavy or something.

424
00:30:25,429 --> 00:30:32,251
[MURDOCK]: It was kind of hard fielding and his throws were just a little bit low or something that normally wouldn't have been.

425
00:30:33,872 --> 00:30:36,693
[MURDOCK]: And Max Carey wrote that one of those errors was

426
00:30:37,117 --> 00:30:38,997
[MURDOCK]: If he hit the ball, it should have been a base hit.

427
00:30:39,017 --> 00:30:41,918
[MURDOCK]: He felt he'd been robbed of the base hit by the scorer.

428
00:30:41,938 --> 00:30:44,438
[MURDOCK]: I just wonder if you did any of those, which Peckinpahl might have done.

429
00:30:44,458 --> 00:30:46,039
[BARNHART]: No, I don't think so.

430
00:30:48,439 --> 00:30:50,439
[MURDOCK]: How was Walter Johnson to hit against?

431
00:30:51,459 --> 00:30:57,640
[BARNHART]: Well, he was pretty speedy.

432
00:30:58,661 --> 00:31:00,981
[BARNHART]: I was considered a curveball hitter.

433
00:31:01,001 --> 00:31:02,961
[BARNHART]: He didn't throw me any curveballs.

434
00:31:03,101 --> 00:31:04,122
[BARNHART]: He was all fastball.

435
00:31:04,162 --> 00:31:05,402
[BARNHART]: He hit me right in the chest.

436
00:31:05,502 --> 00:31:06,002
[MURDOCK]: He hit you?

437
00:31:08,282 --> 00:31:11,143
[MURDOCK]: For you, did you crowd the plate much?

438
00:31:11,263 --> 00:31:13,863
[MURDOCK]: Or did it just get away from him?

439
00:31:15,564 --> 00:31:18,784
[BARNHART]: Just naturally, just trying to pitch inside.

440
00:31:18,804 --> 00:31:26,226
[MURDOCK]: Did you get any hits off him, do you remember?

441
00:31:26,286 --> 00:31:26,546
[BARNHART]: I did.

442
00:31:26,586 --> 00:31:29,427
[MURDOCK]: That's something.

443
00:31:38,286 --> 00:31:39,826
[MURDOCK]: Yeah, we talked to Kovalewski, too.

444
00:31:39,846 --> 00:31:41,067
[MURDOCK]: He was one of those we talked to.

445
00:31:42,227 --> 00:31:43,027
[BARNHART]: You talked to him?

446
00:31:43,047 --> 00:31:43,948
[MURDOCK]: Yes, Stan Kovalewski.

447
00:31:44,348 --> 00:31:45,488
[MURDOCK]: He's out in Indiana.

448
00:31:46,869 --> 00:31:50,370
[WOMAN 1]: I always want to say Big Bend, but that isn't it.

449
00:31:50,730 --> 00:31:51,310
[MURDOCK]: South Bend.

450
00:31:51,370 --> 00:31:52,970
[WOMAN 1]: South Bend, where Notre Dame is.

451
00:31:53,211 --> 00:31:53,471
[MURDOCK]: Yeah.

452
00:31:54,951 --> 00:31:55,631
[MURDOCK]: Loves to fish.

453
00:31:56,111 --> 00:31:56,912
[MURDOCK]: Still goes fishing.

454
00:31:57,712 --> 00:31:59,092
[WOMAN 1]: Yeah, every day.

455
00:32:03,214 --> 00:32:04,374
[WOMAN 1]: He couldn't let him interfere.

456
00:32:04,574 --> 00:32:07,795
[WOMAN 1]: He could be there at a certain time, but he wouldn't interfere with his fishing.

457
00:32:16,272 --> 00:32:20,574
[MURDOCK]: Did you play any ball after you left the Pirates?

458
00:32:22,175 --> 00:32:25,738
[BARNHART]: Yeah, I played in the American Association.

459
00:32:25,758 --> 00:32:27,259
[MURDOCK]: Where did you play with them?

460
00:32:27,959 --> 00:32:28,679
[BARNHART]: Indianapolis.

461
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:32,402
[MURDOCK]: Was Eddie Sicking with Indianapolis?

462
00:32:33,222 --> 00:32:33,783
[BARNHART]: Eddie who?

463
00:32:33,863 --> 00:32:34,223
[MURDOCK]: Sicking.

464
00:32:35,164 --> 00:32:35,844
[MURDOCK]: Second baseman.

465
00:32:36,945 --> 00:32:37,305
[MURDOCK]: Sicking.

466
00:32:39,818 --> 00:32:41,879
[MURDOCK]: He may have come a little later.

467
00:32:41,979 --> 00:32:46,140
[BARNHART]: Is that what they call him?

468
00:32:46,180 --> 00:32:47,521
[BARNHART]: Is that his real name?

469
00:32:48,021 --> 00:32:48,441
[MURDOCK]: Yeah.

470
00:32:48,481 --> 00:32:49,602
[MURDOCK]: S-I-C-K-I-N-G.

471
00:32:51,702 --> 00:32:55,484
[MURDOCK]: He played with Indianapolis at some time, but I don't know if those were the years or not.

472
00:32:55,704 --> 00:32:58,305
[MURDOCK]: He may have been.

473
00:32:58,445 --> 00:33:00,386
[BARNHART]: I can't really say.

474
00:33:00,406 --> 00:33:04,607
[WOMAN 2]: We went to Indianapolis right after that Pittsburgh.

475
00:33:04,627 --> 00:33:05,047
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

476
00:33:05,367 --> 00:33:07,108
[BARNHART]: I went to Pittsburgh, too, I think.

477
00:33:09,954 --> 00:33:13,996
[MURDOCK]: Was that a pretty big disappointment to you when you left the Pirates?

478
00:33:14,936 --> 00:33:15,336
[BARNHART]: Oh, no.

479
00:33:15,536 --> 00:33:20,678
[BARNHART]: I figured I might get back again when I'm out of here.

480
00:33:23,959 --> 00:33:25,460
[WOMAN 2]: You went to Jersey City.

481
00:33:27,281 --> 00:33:30,502
[MURDOCK]: Yeah, then I went to... How long were you in Indianapolis?

482
00:33:31,222 --> 00:33:31,843
[MURDOCK]: A year or two?

483
00:33:31,863 --> 00:33:33,143
[BARNHART]: Two years, I believe.

484
00:33:33,643 --> 00:33:35,024
[MURDOCK]: And then you went to Jersey City?

485
00:33:40,627 --> 00:33:42,468
[MURDOCK]: Well, did you play any night baseball?

486
00:33:44,388 --> 00:33:45,168
[BARNHART]: A couple nights.

487
00:33:48,389 --> 00:33:49,930
[BARNHART]: I didn't.

488
00:33:49,950 --> 00:33:50,550
[MURDOCK]: Where was it?

489
00:33:50,870 --> 00:33:51,590
[MURDOCK]: In Indianapolis?

490
00:33:52,110 --> 00:33:53,210
[BARNHART]: Indianapolis, I believe.

491
00:33:54,291 --> 00:33:55,371
[MURDOCK]: Lights weren't too bright, huh?

492
00:33:56,671 --> 00:33:57,852
[BARNHART]: No, they weren't too good.

493
00:34:08,275 --> 00:34:09,235
[BARNHART]: That's another thing.

494
00:34:11,091 --> 00:34:17,112
[BARNHART]: When I played, I always had my thumbs always bruised up from when I was hitting.

495
00:34:19,213 --> 00:34:21,013
[BARNHART]: That would sting your hands.

496
00:34:22,774 --> 00:34:27,075
[BARNHART]: They wouldn't let you wear gloves then, but everybody can wear gloves now.

497
00:34:28,875 --> 00:34:31,476
[BARNHART]: That would help a batter a whole lot.

498
00:34:33,696 --> 00:34:38,718
[BARNHART]: I used to have a sponge that I'd tape on my bat to get it un-parsed.

499
00:34:45,668 --> 00:34:48,369
[MURDOCK]: How big was your glove compared to those today?

500
00:34:48,989 --> 00:34:50,190
[BARNHART]: Oh, it was just small.

501
00:34:53,351 --> 00:34:54,532
[MURDOCK]: Did you have much padding in it?

502
00:34:55,552 --> 00:34:59,954
[BARNHART]: No, nothing in it.

503
00:35:04,096 --> 00:35:11,119
[MURDOCK]: You think the fielders today with their huge gloves, they catch things they wouldn't have caught in the old days?

504
00:35:11,139 --> 00:35:12,400
[MURDOCK]: Because the gloves are so big.

505
00:35:12,420 --> 00:35:12,900
[BARNHART]: Oh, they might.

506
00:35:14,660 --> 00:35:18,162
[BARNHART]: An awful lot of outfielders only use one hand to catch them all now.

507
00:35:19,263 --> 00:35:25,907
[MURDOCK]: The glove's so big, you can't miss.

508
00:35:26,527 --> 00:35:31,470
[WOMAN 2]: He never used to fall down and roll over and do things like that.

509
00:35:31,490 --> 00:35:34,892
[WOMAN 2]: I don't ever remember seeing them do that when I was at any of the games.

510
00:35:35,613 --> 00:35:37,754
[WOMAN 2]: And I used to go to every one when I was out there.

511
00:35:38,215 --> 00:35:40,716
[MURDOCK]: What was your biggest thrill in seeing him play?

512
00:35:40,736 --> 00:35:41,336
[MURDOCK]: Does anything stand out?

513
00:35:41,356 --> 00:35:41,757
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, I don't know.

514
00:35:41,777 --> 00:35:43,097
[WOMAN 2]: I just sat with my mouth shut.

515
00:35:43,157 --> 00:35:44,878
[WOMAN 2]: I just always liked to see him do.

516
00:35:44,898 --> 00:35:46,679
[WOMAN 2]: If he did all right and if he didn't do all right.

517
00:35:46,699 --> 00:35:48,040
[MURDOCK]: Did the wives sit together?

518
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:51,942
[WOMAN 2]: I started it, but I sat downstairs.

519
00:35:52,022 --> 00:35:53,082
[WOMAN 2]: I sat up in the box.

520
00:35:54,523 --> 00:35:59,745
[WOMAN 1]: You and Mrs. Kovalevski, she said she never wanted to sit near any of the other players' wives.

521
00:36:00,225 --> 00:36:02,547
[WOMAN 1]: She'd go off and sit in the stand by herself.

522
00:36:05,208 --> 00:36:07,409
[SPEAKER 01]: There was a lot of the wives.

523
00:36:08,947 --> 00:36:15,591
[BARNHART]: get in arguments about their husbands' best play or something.

524
00:36:15,871 --> 00:36:19,594
[WOMAN 2]: I never opened my mouth.

525
00:36:19,694 --> 00:36:21,215
[WOMAN 2]: I went to watch the games when I was born.

526
00:36:21,235 --> 00:36:22,255
[MURDOCK]: Did you enjoy the games?

527
00:36:22,395 --> 00:36:23,196
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, yes.

528
00:36:23,836 --> 00:36:24,757
[WOMAN 2]: You believe I did.

529
00:36:24,817 --> 00:36:28,039
[WOMAN 2]: I went to both World Series.

530
00:36:31,021 --> 00:36:32,221
[MURDOCK]: How'd you two happen to meet?

531
00:36:33,102 --> 00:36:35,343
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, we lived about a mile within each other.

532
00:36:36,084 --> 00:36:38,005
[WOMAN 2]: We lived in Buck Valley, Pennsylvania.

533
00:36:42,594 --> 00:36:45,676
[MURDOCK]: So you knew each other from, really, the early days?

534
00:36:47,537 --> 00:36:54,521
[WOMAN 2]: We didn't go to the same country school, but they had different country schools.

535
00:36:55,361 --> 00:37:04,246
[WOMAN 2]: You didn't live too far apart, what was it, down the line?

536
00:37:11,648 --> 00:37:13,148
[MURDOCK]: Did you ever play in a no-hit game?

537
00:37:14,589 --> 00:37:14,729
[BARNHART]: No.

538
00:37:17,589 --> 00:37:21,810
[MURDOCK]: Did any pirates, pitchers, pitch any no-hitters?

539
00:37:30,733 --> 00:37:34,133
[WOMAN 2]: We never talked baseball after he left the park, after a bit.

540
00:37:35,554 --> 00:37:36,534
[MURDOCK]: Even after a good day?

541
00:37:37,334 --> 00:37:38,374
[WOMAN 2]: Even after a good day.

542
00:37:39,295 --> 00:37:41,135
[WOMAN 2]: The only thing I remember about him was

543
00:37:42,151 --> 00:37:49,314
[WOMAN 2]: If we went anyplace, you know, he always went to the ballpark before I did.

544
00:37:49,954 --> 00:37:52,075
[WOMAN 2]: And every hairpin he saw, he picked up.

545
00:37:52,315 --> 00:37:54,516
[WOMAN 2]: That was a two-base hit for me.

546
00:37:54,536 --> 00:37:55,056
[WOMAN 1]: Oh, really?

547
00:37:55,696 --> 00:37:57,277
[WOMAN 2]: And that was his superstition.

548
00:37:57,577 --> 00:37:59,178
[WOMAN 2]: And it worked pretty good, too.

549
00:37:59,358 --> 00:38:00,318
[BARNHART]: Isn't that something?

550
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:07,041
[MURDOCK]: So baseball players are superstitious, huh?

551
00:38:07,961 --> 00:38:09,642
[BARNHART]: Well, we're all superstitious.

552
00:38:10,917 --> 00:38:18,584
[MURDOCK]: Don't step on the foul line or anything.

553
00:38:21,026 --> 00:38:21,607
[MURDOCK]: Not at all?

554
00:38:21,667 --> 00:38:23,008
[MURDOCK]: You never talked baseball?

555
00:38:23,028 --> 00:38:23,208
[WOMAN 2]: No.

556
00:38:23,268 --> 00:38:25,090
[WOMAN 2]: Not after he got home, no.

557
00:38:25,130 --> 00:38:33,077
[WOMAN 1]: Well, he had finished his day's work and that was that.

558
00:38:33,757 --> 00:38:36,340
[MURDOCK]: Well, I mean, he'd gotten three doubles or something.

559
00:38:37,484 --> 00:38:38,484
[MURDOCK]: You didn't talk about it?

560
00:38:38,504 --> 00:38:41,846
[WOMAN 2]: No, I didn't say it was a good day, because he had a good many good days.

561
00:38:41,906 --> 00:38:44,927
[WOMAN 2]: I know it, and he knows it, so I thought about it.

562
00:38:45,027 --> 00:38:54,291
[WOMAN 2]: One of his clippings, I remember it.

563
00:38:54,351 --> 00:38:59,272
[WOMAN 2]: They said, when he came in from right field, he neither looked right nor left.

564
00:39:00,533 --> 00:39:03,694
[WOMAN 2]: The grandstands, anybody, he just walked right straight ahead.

565
00:39:03,794 --> 00:39:05,835
[WOMAN 2]: I always called him old eagle-eye.

566
00:39:15,881 --> 00:39:24,948
[MURDOCK]: It sounds like you didn't get very nervous or excited during the games from what I gather here, is that true?

567
00:39:26,228 --> 00:39:27,389
[WOMAN 2]: I don't know.

568
00:39:27,429 --> 00:39:28,570
[WOMAN 1]: You and me both.

569
00:39:32,633 --> 00:39:33,954
[WOMAN 2]: I think I need to have a look at it.

570
00:39:34,174 --> 00:39:35,435
[WOMAN 1]: I don't see how...

571
00:39:36,956 --> 00:39:38,277
[WOMAN 1]: I think if I were a wife,

572
00:39:38,622 --> 00:39:39,723
[WOMAN 1]: and my husband was playing.

573
00:39:39,743 --> 00:39:42,605
[BARNHART]: Well, I'd have to go off in a corner by myself and watch the game.

574
00:39:42,665 --> 00:39:44,186
[WOMAN 2]: No, I didn't mind a bit.

575
00:39:44,366 --> 00:39:44,606
[WOMAN 1]: Really?

576
00:39:44,626 --> 00:39:46,187
[WOMAN 1]: Oh, I would be a nervous wreck.

577
00:39:47,048 --> 00:39:48,929
[MURDOCK]: Did you ever see him get hit, for example?

578
00:39:49,350 --> 00:39:51,071
[WOMAN 2]: No, I didn't see him get hit that day.

579
00:39:51,111 --> 00:39:51,771
[WOMAN 2]: He broke his elbow.

580
00:39:51,791 --> 00:39:52,712
[WOMAN 2]: Oh, man.

581
00:39:52,732 --> 00:39:54,493
[MURDOCK]: That was by a pitched ball?

582
00:39:55,694 --> 00:39:56,435
[WOMAN 2]: I guess it was.

583
00:39:57,776 --> 00:40:00,878
[WOMAN 2]: He was home for a little while.

584
00:40:00,998 --> 00:40:01,658
[WOMAN 2]: Where did you go?

585
00:40:01,678 --> 00:40:04,560
[WOMAN 2]: Where did they send you to get your arm set?

586
00:40:04,600 --> 00:40:05,501
[MURDOCK]: Home setter reached?

587
00:40:06,081 --> 00:40:06,582
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

588
00:40:07,192 --> 00:40:08,733
[WOMAN 2]: Was it home setter Reese?

589
00:40:09,793 --> 00:40:11,174
[WOMAN 2]: Or was it Youngstown?

590
00:40:11,194 --> 00:40:12,294
[MURDOCK]: He was in Youngstown.

591
00:40:12,394 --> 00:40:14,715
[BARNHART]: I don't remember what the...

592
00:40:16,596 --> 00:40:18,296
[WOMAN 2]: But he came home for a while.

593
00:40:18,316 --> 00:40:21,958
[BARNHART]: So lost for a while, too.

594
00:40:25,179 --> 00:40:27,000
[BARNHART]: There wasn't much setting to do.

595
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:28,660
[BARNHART]: I believe so.

596
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:30,321
[MURDOCK]: It was your throwing arm, huh?

597
00:40:30,341 --> 00:40:34,283
[MURDOCK]: Did you have any trouble?

598
00:40:34,303 --> 00:40:35,283
[BARNHART]: With my broken arm.

599
00:40:36,976 --> 00:40:38,877
[MURDOCK]: When was that, in 1922?

600
00:40:41,998 --> 00:40:42,958
[MURDOCK]: Maybe 26, huh?

601
00:40:44,619 --> 00:40:45,439
[MURDOCK]: That was a good year.

602
00:40:45,459 --> 00:40:50,581
[MURDOCK]: He played 76 games.

603
00:40:50,601 --> 00:40:54,682
[MURDOCK]: Did you have trouble throwing after that?

604
00:40:54,742 --> 00:40:55,683
[MURDOCK]: Did you come back?

605
00:40:55,823 --> 00:40:56,643
[BARNHART]: It took a while.

606
00:40:56,703 --> 00:40:57,303
[BARNHART]: It took a while.

607
00:41:14,025 --> 00:41:22,607
[WOMAN 2]: And we had a Christmas card from Tom Shane two years ago, and he wrote on it that we've been playing, you know, now we've been millionaires.

608
00:41:22,627 --> 00:41:23,287
[WOMAN 1]: That's right.

609
00:41:23,327 --> 00:41:23,987
[WOMAN 1]: That's right.

610
00:41:28,788 --> 00:41:30,508
[MURDOCK]: Some of them are pretty bitter about that.

611
00:41:30,528 --> 00:41:37,330
[MURDOCK]: I mean, how do you feel about these modern players and their salaries as against their abilities?

612
00:41:39,410 --> 00:41:41,511
[MURDOCK]: I mean, $4.3 million contracts.

613
00:41:48,492 --> 00:41:55,654
[WOMAN 2]: A lot of them did a lot of good, though, for, you know, other charities and things.

614
00:41:57,955 --> 00:42:03,377
[BARNHART]: Tom sent me a Christmas card every year at Pittsburgh.

615
00:42:03,477 --> 00:42:05,218
[WOMAN 2]: Two years ago?

616
00:42:05,238 --> 00:42:06,498
[BARNHART]: Just two years ago.

617
00:42:06,618 --> 00:42:13,260
[BARNHART]: I don't know whether he died or... Well, we'll have to find out.

618
00:42:14,541 --> 00:42:18,282
[WOMAN 2]: Mr. Murdoch said he never heard that he died, so he must still be living.

619
00:42:18,631 --> 00:42:19,972
[MURDOCK]: Yeah, I think he is.

620
00:42:19,992 --> 00:42:25,514
[WOMAN 2]: He could be sick or something, you know.

621
00:42:25,534 --> 00:42:26,895
[BARNHART]: He lived in California.

622
00:42:29,436 --> 00:42:30,997
[MURDOCK]: Did he play with the pirates then?

623
00:42:31,877 --> 00:42:33,218
[BARNHART]: Pardon?

624
00:42:33,438 --> 00:42:35,419
[MURDOCK]: Did he play with the Pirates when you were there?

625
00:42:35,959 --> 00:42:36,359
[BARNHART]: Yeah.

626
00:42:40,882 --> 00:42:47,205
[BARNHART]: We got to come back and correct the job.

627
00:43:21,878 --> 00:43:27,983
[WOMAN 1]: And he said at one time they were all broken.

628
00:43:29,004 --> 00:43:31,806
[MURDOCK]: Do you have any pictures of yourself?

629
00:43:32,867 --> 00:43:35,349
[MURDOCK]: Scrapbooks or clippings of your playing days?

630
00:43:36,489 --> 00:43:37,890
[BARNHART]: Oh, I have a scrapbook.

631
00:43:37,971 --> 00:43:39,592
[BARNHART]: A scrapbook about that big.

632
00:43:39,752 --> 00:43:40,332
[MURDOCK]: Is that right?

633
00:43:41,873 --> 00:43:43,815
[WOMAN 2]: That's where they had him that time.

634
00:43:43,875 --> 00:43:47,618
[MURDOCK]: Suppose I could take a look at it.

635
00:43:48,298 --> 00:43:50,480
[WOMAN 2]: All night and all day and all morning.

636
00:43:51,099 --> 00:43:53,141
[WOMAN 1]: Oh, really?

637
00:43:53,181 --> 00:43:54,662
[WOMAN 2]: Yeah, it did.

638
00:43:54,722 --> 00:43:56,943
[WOMAN 2]: One was from New York, I think, yesterday.

639
00:43:57,684 --> 00:44:00,566
[WOMAN 2]: And it was a long envelope.

640
00:44:01,486 --> 00:44:03,708
[WOMAN 2]: And you know what it had in it?

641
00:44:03,728 --> 00:44:04,708
[WOMAN 2]: Just a postcard.

642
00:44:05,189 --> 00:44:06,550
[WOMAN 2]: A pen and paper email.

643
00:44:06,610 --> 00:44:07,831
[WOMAN 2]: I mean, they're 10 cents.

644
00:44:07,991 --> 00:44:10,592
[WOMAN 2]: I hadn't bought any, and I said, well, I looked at it.

645
00:44:10,612 --> 00:44:13,014
[WOMAN 2]: I wanted to know how much it was, and it was 10 cents.

646
00:44:14,115 --> 00:44:15,696
[WOMAN 2]: And this last three autographs.

